content.text:
At 1280x720 the page is scrolled and the verdict action dock is below the fold. This is a problem for "buttons visible" especially on laptop screens. Need to ensure verdict controls are visible without scrolling on desktop. Options:

1. Make the docket panel sticky with internal scroll area and fixed action dock, with height constrained to viewport. E.g. at desktop, set `.app {height:100dvh; overflow:hidden}`, `.main{flex:1; min-height:0}`, `.docket-panel{max-height:100%}`, `.docket-scroll{overflow-y:auto; flex:1}`, `.action-dock{flex:0 0 auto}`. Then the scene column needs to fit; if scene canvas is bigger than available height, it may overflow/clipped. We can size scene to available height and width. Use CSS container sizing:
   - `.scene-column {min-height:0; display:flex; flex-direction:column;}`
   - `.scene-frame {width:100%;}` would compute 16:9 height maybe 405 at 1280 (left width 721 => 405). Available main height ~ 720-100-? = 600; scene+track+notes+record = 405+34+58+92+27=616 >600. Slight overflow. If we hide record on shorter screens, it fits: 405+34+58+18=515. At 1280x720, main available height ~ 720 - 24 padding - 62 HUD - 10 gap - ~0 footer = 624. Scene 405 + track 34 + notes 58 + gaps 18 = 515, fits. So at max-height <= 800, hide `.record-panel` and maybe footer, and let app height 100dvh with docket internal scroll. Action dock pinned.
   - Docket content will scroll internally; at 720 height, docket available height 624, action dock ~164, scroll area 460 — enough to show most content but maybe needs scroll to see evidence fully. Acceptable; verdict controls always visible. This is much better.

2. Alternative: make docket action dock position sticky at bottom of viewport while page scrolls. But docket panel is tall; action dock at its bottom only becomes visible when scrolled. Could use `position: fixed` at bottom? Then it would cover page content and always visible. On desktop, this could work but overlays footer/content. Maybe a floating verdict bar fixed bottom-right with the docket width; content scrolls behind. That is common in games. But could look like a HUD. We can make `.action-dock { position: sticky; bottom: 0 }` within docket panel; but since the page is scrolled and docket panel is taller than viewport, sticky bottom doesn't make it appear until its normal position reaches bottom. Actually sticky with `bottom:0` sticks when the element would move below the bottom of the viewport; for an element at the bottom of a taller container, when you're at scrollTop 0 its normal position is below viewport - it does not stick upwards. So no.

Hence option 1: viewport-fit desktop with internal scroll. Let's implement carefully.

Need to ensure scene + left column fit. At 1280x720, if app height 100dvh and overflow hidden, scene area needs to fit. Hide record at max-height 800 (or always? record is nice at 900). Could show record when height >= 850 only. Bench notes are 58; could hide at max-height 760 to save. Let's map:

Available: app height 720; padding top 12 + bottom 10 => 698; header ~62 + gap 10 => 626 main. Page footer hidden in fit mode.
Scene column: scene frame width? main grid: content width 1280-24=1256; gap 16; columns 1.45:0.95 => left 755, right 485. Scene height 755/1.6=472. That already exceeds 626 minus track 34 minus notes 58 = 534 -> 472+34+58+18=582 fits 626. Record hidden => fits. Good. Wait current screenshot left scene is from y104 to 568 => 464 height, yes 721 width/1.6=451? Actually 721? Grid left maybe 721. Whatever, fits.

At 1024x600: left width ~575 => scene 359; header 60; main height 600-? 520; scene+34+58=451 fits. Good.
At 1440x900: left width ~824 => 515; main available ~900-24-70-16=790; scene+34+58+record~? 515+34+58+108+27=742 fits. Show record.
At 1280x900 similarly.

So set:
```
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .app {height:100dvh; overflow:hidden;}
  .main {flex:1; min-height:0;}
  .scene-column {min-height:0;}
  .docket-panel {min-height:0; height:100%;}
  .docket-scroll {flex:1; overflow-y:auto; min-height:0;}
  .action-dock, .result-panel {flex-shrink:0;}
  .page-foot {display:none?} maybe keep if space; hide to maximize.
  .record-panel {flex:1; min-height:0;} record will stretch
}
```
But if .scene-column content exceeds main height, it overflows/clips; hide record/notes on shorter screens:
```
@media (min-width:1024px) and (max-height:820px) and (min-width:1024px) {
  .record-panel {display:none;}
  .bench-notes {display:none;}
  .scene-frame {max-height: calc(100% - 48px); width:auto; ...}
}
```
Need scene frame to shrink if needed. The scene width currently fixed 100% of column, height 16:9. At 1024x600: left column width? content 1000, gap 16, ratio 1.45+0.95=2.4 => 594. Scene height 371. track 34 => 405, fits 600-? ~500. Fine. At 1440x720: left 824 => 515; main height ~620; scene+34 = 549, fits; notes hidden. Good. At 1920x1080: left ~1100 => 687; main ~960; scene+34+58+record fits.

The issue is at 1280x720, scene 472 + track 34 = 506, fits 626. Great.

Docket scroll: at 1280x720, scroll area 626 - action dock (~164) = 462. Content: heading ~90 + defendant 78 + case card ~160 + evidence ~260 + guidelines 30 + gaps = ~640. So user scrolls within docket to read evidence fully; the action dock is always visible. That's acceptable and better than page scrolling. But the case summary/evidence may not all be visible initially; the screenshot will show a truncated evidence card. The user can scroll. We could compress the docket to fit more at 720 by reducing font sizes / padding on short heights. But the panel internal scroll is fine. To make the initial screen show witness/evidence (the core), maybe make heading/defendant more compact at short height. Could also allow docket scroll but the evidence is important; it's just below fold. Hmm. At 720, first screen would show heading, defendant, case card partially, maybe evidence not visible. The player must scroll. That's acceptable for laptops but ideally the evidence is visible. We could add a hint "scroll for the full case file" or make the docket more compact universally.

Let's inspect at 1280x720 after implementing internal scroll. We can adjust.

Alternative: reduce scene height and use the saved vertical space for the docket. We could set grid ratio bigger left? No, smaller left = shorter scene but narrower. At 1280, reduce scene to ~400px by limiting max-height while keeping width would distort; could letterbox canvas with `object-fit: contain` on a container. Better: at short viewports, set `.scene-frame { max-width: 640px; }` making it smaller and align center; docket gets no benefit because column width fixed, but left height lower leaving space; docket still scrolls. Docket width fixed 485. We could increase right column width at short viewports by changing grid ratio to 1.2fr 1fr, giving docket more width (and therefore less height). At 1280: left 665 -> scene 416; right 575 -> more room for text, shorter content. That helps. Could set `@media (max-height: 800px) { .main {grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(420px,1fr);} }`. Docket 575 wide -> evidence cards wider, fewer lines, saving 50px. Scene 416+34=450 fits. Nice.

At 1280x720, docket width 575. Content: heading ~90, def 78, case ~130, evidence maybe 220, guidelines 30, gaps 40 = 588. Scroll area 462, still scroll ~126. Could compress spacing on short heights:
- reduce docket-scroll gap to 8, padding 10
- heading title 22px, margins smaller
- case summary font 13px
- evidence detail font 11px
- witness text 12px
- card padding 8.
This could get to ~500. Still slight scroll. Fine.

Do we need everything visible at once? Not strictly. The verdict controls are what must be visible. We'll ensure they are pinned. The case file scroll is acceptable in a game UI.

But at 1280x720 the current page scroll shows the verdict controls completely below. We must fix.

Implement desktop viewport fit. Need be careful at widths 981-1023 (tablet landscape) we keep page scroll layout.

CSS:
```
@media (min-width: 1000px) {
  .app { height:100dvh; overflow:hidden; }
  .main { flex:1; min-height:0; }
  .scene-column { min-height:0; }
  .docket-panel { min-height:0; height:100%; }
  .docket-scroll { flex:1; min-height:0; overflow-y:auto; }
  .action-dock, .result-panel { flex-shrink:0; }
  .page-foot { display:none; }
  .record-panel { min-height:0; }
}
```
At widths >=1000 but heights very short (e.g. 600), scene might overflow. Add:
```
@media (min-width:1000px) and (max-height:830px) {
  .bench-notes {display:none;}
  .record-panel {display:none;}
  .main {grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1.22fr) minmax(420px,1fr);}
  .docket-scroll {padding:10px 12px; gap:8px;}
  ...
}
```
At 1280x720 with new ratio: content 1256-16=1240; left 1240*1.22/2.22=681; scene 426; right 559. Good. Action dock 164; scroll 626-164=462. Docket content compact might be ~560. Slight internal scroll.

But at 1024x720: content 1000-16=984; left 541 (scene 338), right 443. Docket scroll ~462. Fine.
At 1024x600: scene 330; fit main height ~500. Left 330+34=364 fits. Good.
At 1000x500: main height ~400; scene 325+34=359 fits; header 60. Tight but works.
At 1440x900: media max-height 830 doesn't apply, so record and notes show; app height fit; left scene 515+34+58+record 108+gaps=742, main available ~790; fine. docket panel height 100%; content 840-ish > available 790; scroll internal. Actually docket previously ended 858 with page at 900; with fit mode, scroll area clips content and actions pinned. At 1440x900 it would show a scrollbar in docket. That's okay but maybe not ideal compared to current full fit. We can check: main available = 900 - pad 24 - header 62 - gap 16 = 798. Docket action dock 164 + scroll content (heading 90 + defendant 78 + case 160 + evidence 250 + guidelines 30 + gaps 40 + padding 24) = 672 -> total 836 > 798. So scroll needed ~38. Slight. Could compress or accept. The page no longer scrolls; docket scroll internal. Fine.

But the scene left width at 1440 remains 824; scene 515 + track 34 + notes 58 + record 108 + gaps 27 = 742 <798. Fits.

At 1280x720, scene ratio narrower? okay.

Need `.record-panel` flex:1 and min-height 0 inside scene column; if not enough space it shrinks below content and hides overflow. With max-height 830 we hide it.

Let's implement. Also maybe hide `.bench-notes` only at max-height 780; at 1280x720 hidden. At 1440x768, height 768 -> hidden; scene 515? left width may 824 -> 515; main height 670; scene+track=549; fits with notes? 549+58=607; fit. Could keep notes. Let's use max-height 780 for hiding notes; max-height 830 for record. At 768, record hidden, notes shown, scene fits 670. Docket scroll height 670-164=506. Better.

At 1280x720: main avail ~626, scene at ratio 1.22 -> 426+34=460; notes 58 => 527 fits. So we could show notes; hide record only. But docket benefits from compact. Keep notes maybe to fill. At 720, left has space below notes 626-527=99; could show record compressed 99? Record min-height 92 could show. Hmm but this would reduce available? Scene column content can overflow; record flex:1 with min-height 92 and app overflow hidden may be clipped at bottom. Better hide record at <=830. Notes show. Left ends 527, leaving ~100 dark empty; acceptable, or center content vertically? Not crucial.

Let's set:
```
@media (min-width:1000px) {
  app height 100dvh overflow hidden; main flex:1 min-height:0; 
  docket height 100% min-height 0; docket-scroll flex:1 overflow-y:auto;
  footer display none;
  record min-height:0;
