Evidence modal looks great. The receipt icon rendered as gold/cream horizontal bars maybe not obviously a receipt, but it's fine. It appears like a document. Good. The modal is sharp, textured. The background blur works. Great.

Now test game end. Need play through 10 cases or hack? We should test end game flow properly. Could automate clicking through cases by picking options according to truth, using data not exposed. But we can manually simulate with UI. However to test all cases and endgame, we can add a temporary debug script through the browser? Module state is not exposed. We can drive UI: for each case, inspect title via DOM, click verdict, choose appropriate sentence (if guilty), confirm, wait, click next. We as AI know the cases. Automate via Playwright eval with async? `eval` can run an async function with awaits maybe. Or use repeated CLI commands.

Simplest: Use playwright_cli eval to execute a script that programmatically clicks and waits using promises, and sequences decisions based on case titles. We can define a JS async function in page context:
```
(async () => {
  const wait = ms => new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,ms));
  const answers = { 'The Stolen Bicycle': ['guilty','fine'], ... };
  for (let k=0;k<10;k++){
    const title = document.querySelector('#caseTitle').textContent;
    const [v,s] = answers[title];
    document.querySelector(v==='guilty'?'#guiltyBtn':'#innocentBtn').click();
    if (s) document.querySelector(`[data-sentence=${s}]`).click();
    document.querySelector('#confirmBtn').click();
    await wait(1300);
    document.querySelector('#nextBtn').click();
    await wait(600);
  }
  return {title: document.querySelector('#finalRank').textContent, ...}
})()
```
